From: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] zebra: make the EVPN MH local-inactive decisions unit testable

The preceding patches decide when a MAC or a neighbour stops being locally
active and when it may be retired. Every bug they fix was an ordering bug
rather than a wrong rule, so the rules themselves were never pinned by
anything: reaching these states on hardware needs an Ethernet Segment, two
PEs and expired hold timers.

Split the decisions out into zebra/zebra_evpn_mh_decisions.h, kept free of
zebra types, and call them from the paths that used to inline them:
zebra_evpn_local_neigh_update() in both of its branches, the two neighbour
walkers, the MAC update that hands neighbours between them,
zebra_evpn_mac_is_static() and zebra_evpn_mac_reeval_local_inactive().

No behaviour change: each helper is the condition that was already there.
What it buys is that the tests exercise the code that runs, not a copy of
it, and that the two duplicated neighbour paths are now provably the same
rule rather than two transcriptions of it.

tests/zebra/test_zebra_evpn_mh_decisions.c covers the cases the hardware
tests take ten minutes each to reach, including the interleave that left a
departed MAC advertised forever: the peer flags clear, the MAC delete is
evaluated while a sync neighbour reference is still held, and only then does
the reference count reach zero.

Signed-off-by: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
---
